1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:03,649
The month you have the least money is the month you handle it worst.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,820
You miss the payment. You forget the appointment.

3
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:09,260
You buy the thing you cannot afford.

4
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:12,710
And you already have the explanation. You are bad with money.

5
00:00:12,790 --> 00:00:17,960
Undisciplined. Short-sighted. But when researchers measured it, the story inverted.

6
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:20,980
Being short of money does not reveal a worse mind.

7
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:25,630
It produces one, by taking a resource that researchers call bandwidth,

8
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,860
which is the mental capacity you have left over for everything else.

9
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:34,280
In the next few minutes you will see that capacity measured in the same people twice,

10
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,180
a few months apart, and you will see exactly where it went.

11
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:41,880
Start in a shopping mall in New Jersey. Anandi Mani,

12
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:47,000
Sendhil Mullainathan, Eldar Shafir and Jiaying Zhao

13
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:51,700
stopped ordinary shoppers and asked them to imagine one small problem.

14
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:57,080
Your car needs a repair. For half the people the repair costs a hundred and fifty dollars.

15
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:01,080
For the other half the same repair costs fifteen hundred.

16
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:05,880
Hold that in mind, they said. Then, before asking anyone what they would do about it,

17
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:08,320
they gave everyone a standard reasoning test.

18
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,920
Shapes and patterns. Nothing to do with cars or money.

19
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,950
When the repair was cheap, the wealthier shoppers and the poorer shoppers scored about the same.

20
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:21,610
When the repair was expensive, the wealthier shoppers scored about the same as before.

21
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:26,120
The poorer shoppers dropped. Nothing had actually happened to anyone.

22
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:30,000
No money left any account. It was an imaginary car.

23
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:33,300
But for the shoppers who could not have found fifteen hundred dollars,

24
00:01:33,380 --> 00:01:38,140
the question was not imaginary at all, and some part of the mind went off to work on it and had

25
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:43,920
not come back by the time the shapes appeared. The test did not measure how smart anyone was.

26
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:48,320
It measured what was left over. That is what bandwidth means.

27
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:51,020
Not intelligence. Capacity.

28
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:56,340
The part of your mind that is free right now, this second, to hold a plan,

29
00:01:56,540 --> 00:02:01,770
resist an impulse, notice a date, and follow a sentence to the end of itself.

30
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:06,680
It is a fixed amount, and anything running underneath is spending it.

31
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:09,520
A tune stuck in your head spends a little.

32
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:12,100
A number you cannot cover spends a great deal,

33
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:17,120
because your mind is not built to file an unsolved survival problem and move on.

34
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:20,760
It keeps working on it. Quietly.

35
00:02:21,470 --> 00:02:26,520
Without asking you. The mall study proved the effect existed.

36
00:02:26,829 --> 00:02:29,940
The next one proved it was not about the people.

37
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:35,380
The same researchers went to Tamil Nadu, in southern India, and found sugarcane farmers,

38
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:39,240
who are paid once a year. In the month before the harvest

39
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:45,120
they are genuinely poor. Loans out, pawn tickets in the drawer, bills waiting.

40
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:48,840
In the month after the harvest the same money problem is simply gone.

41
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,820
Same farmer. Same field. Same brain.

42
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:55,710
Roughly four hundred and fifty of them were tested at both moments.

43
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:59,020
After the harvest, they scored meaningfully higher.

44
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:03,700
The researchers put the gap at somewhere around thirteen points on an IQ test,

45
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:06,260
and to give you a sense of what that size means,

46
00:03:06,340 --> 00:03:10,960
it is in the range of what a full night without sleep does to your thinking.

47
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:14,340
Read that again, because the design of it is the whole argument.

48
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:17,400
Not poor people compared with rich people.

49
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:20,900
The same person, compared with himself, in two different months.

50
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:22,860
Whatever you might want to say about character,

51
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:25,880
discipline or upbringing is held perfectly still,

52
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:28,800
because it is the same character in both columns.

53
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:31,680
Only the money moved. And it was not hunger

54
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:35,060
and it was not exhaustion. They checked.

55
00:03:35,450 --> 00:03:40,000
The pre-harvest farmers were not doing harder physical work or eating worse in any way that

56
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:43,180
explained it. So there was a mind that scored higher,

57
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:47,260
sitting inside the same skull the whole time, unreachable for a few months.

58
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:50,960
Where does it go? It goes into the tunnel.

59
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:55,300
Anuj Shah, working with Mullainathan and Shafir,

60
00:03:55,540 --> 00:03:58,380
ran a version of this in a lab using games.

61
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:02,100
Some players got plenty of guesses and some got very few,

62
00:04:02,410 --> 00:04:05,650
which made them rich or poor inside that little world.

63
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:07,840
The poor players were not sloppy.

64
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:12,920
They were the opposite. They concentrated harder on each guess and got more out of it than the

65
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:17,740
rich players did. Then the researchers let people borrow guesses from future rounds.

66
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,840
The poor players borrowed, and borrowed again,

67
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:25,880
and finished with worse total scores than if they had never been allowed to borrow at all.

68
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:30,980
That is not carelessness. That is focus, aimed at the wrong distance.

69
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:34,420
Scarcity does not blur your attention, it narrows it,

70
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:37,500
and everything outside the narrow part goes dark.

71
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:41,850
Next week is outside. The letter you have not opened is outside.

72
00:04:41,930 --> 00:04:45,660
The cheaper option that only pays off in three months is outside.

73
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:50,280
Inside the tunnel there is one urgent thing and you are extremely good at it,

74
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:53,780
and the bill for all the things you could not see arrives later,

75
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:55,840
and looks exactly like a character flaw.

76
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:59,320
Now go back far enough and this stops looking like a design fault.

77
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:03,140
For most of human existence you lived in a group of a few dozen people,

78
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:07,720
and Marshall Sahlins made an argument about those groups that still unsettles economists.

79
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:11,540
He said affluence is not an amount, it is a ratio,

80
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:15,060
the distance between what you want and what you can reach.

81
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,800
Foragers closed that gap from the other side.

82
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:22,520
They wanted little, they got enough, and they worked a manageable number of hours to do it.

83
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:24,960
Not paradise. Not desperate either.

84
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:29,080
The reason they were not desperate is the part that matters for you.

85
00:05:29,160 --> 00:05:32,640
James Woodburn described these as immediate-return societies.

86
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:35,580
You got food today and it was eaten within a day or two.

87
00:05:35,760 --> 00:05:39,339
There was almost no storage, no granary, no savings,

88
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:43,420
and tools were simple enough to remake. Wealth did not pile up,

89
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:45,820
and in most of these groups it was not allowed to.

90
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:48,980
Someone who started accumulating was mocked back into line.

91
00:05:49,060 --> 00:05:51,780
There is a practical reason sitting underneath the moral one.

92
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:56,070
You carried what you owned. A group that follows the food moves camp several times a year,

93
00:05:56,150 --> 00:05:59,260
and every possession is a thing somebody has to pick up and walk with,

94
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:02,260
so a pile of stored goods is not security, it is weight.

95
00:06:02,450 --> 00:06:06,880
That is Sahlins again. The forager is not poor because he owns almost nothing.

96
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:09,820
He owns almost nothing because owning more would slow him down,

97
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:14,380
and being slow was the real danger. So what did you do when there was nothing to eat tomorrow?

98
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:19,050
You asked. Nicolas Peterson described this as demand sharing.

99
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:23,280
In many foraging societies the transfer does not begin with a generous giver,

100
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:26,220
it begins with the person who needs it walking over and asking,

101
00:06:26,300 --> 00:06:30,520
and the asking is entirely legitimate. Notice what that does to the shame.

102
00:06:30,660 --> 00:06:34,240
The strange act in that system is not asking, it is refusing.

103
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:38,180
Nobody is admired for handing over meat, because handing over meat is what the meat is for.

104
00:06:38,260 --> 00:06:41,400
And the storage problem gets solved without a single container.

105
00:06:41,480 --> 00:06:46,400
Your surplus sits inside thirty other people, and every one of them remembers the balance.

106
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:50,970
Now put the tunnel back into that world and watch it work perfectly.

107
00:06:51,050 --> 00:06:55,790
The stakes were not abstract. A bad stretch meant three or four days of very little,

108
00:06:55,870 --> 00:07:01,180
in a body carrying almost nothing in reserve, in a place with no shop and no next paycheck.

109
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:04,790
Children died in the gap between one kill and the next.

110
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:10,140
So a shortage was the single most important fact in the world for as long as it lasted,

111
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:13,940
and a mind that could not stop turning it over was not malfunctioning.

112
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:16,180
It was doing the only job that mattered.

113
00:07:16,260 --> 00:07:20,560
And the shortage was acute. It lasted days, not decades.

114
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:23,820
It was concrete. You could point at the empty thing.

115
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:28,280
And it had an exit, and the exit was a person, someone with a name,

116
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:33,220
who was standing about thirty feet away, who owed you or would owe you.

117
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:39,460
So a mind that dropped everything else and drove hard at the shortage was a mind that got fed.

118
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:45,380
Tunneling was not a bug. It was the correct emergency response to a solvable emergency,

119
00:07:45,460 --> 00:07:50,340
and the emergency ended. That is the version of you the wiring was built for.

120
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:54,800
Now look at what happens when you run it here. Modern shortage is not acute.

121
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:59,680
It is chronic. It is not a bad week between kills, it is the same gap every month,

122
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:02,200
arriving on a schedule, for years.

123
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:07,400
The alarm that was designed to fire hard and then switch off never gets the signal to switch

124
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:12,040
off, so the tunnel opens and simply stays open.

125
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:15,160
Your bandwidth is not being taxed during a crisis.

126
00:08:15,240 --> 00:08:17,820
It is being taxed as a permanent condition.

127
00:08:17,900 --> 00:08:23,280
The exit is not a person either. It is a rate, a form, a waiting list,

128
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:26,880
a wage set by somebody you will never meet.

129
00:08:26,960 --> 00:08:29,590
There is nobody thirty feet away to ask.

130
00:08:29,670 --> 00:08:34,130
And asking has been recoded from a normal act into an admission of failure,

131
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:40,180
so the one move your ancestors made without embarrassment is the move you would rather starve

132
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:44,860
than make. When did you last ask anyone for anything that actually mattered?

133
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:48,000
Then the system does something genuinely cruel.

134
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:53,800
It charges you for the tunnel. Every failure that narrowed attention produces has a fee attached

135
00:08:53,880 --> 00:08:57,360
to it. Miss the date, pay the late charge.

136
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:00,120
Miss the balance, pay the overdraft.

137
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:03,200
Miss the deadline, pay the reconnection.

138
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:08,660
Buy the cheap one twice because you could not reach for the one that lasts.

139
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:13,400
Every one of those is priced, and every one of them is more likely when you are short,

140
00:09:13,480 --> 00:09:16,480
which means being short of money is itself expensive,

141
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:20,860
and the expense arrives dressed as evidence about the kind of person you are.

142
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:24,560
You are inside a loop where the shortage takes the capacity,

143
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:29,430
the missing capacity produces the mistakes, and the mistakes deepen the shortage.

144
00:09:29,510 --> 00:09:34,620
And the whole time, the only explanation you are handed is the one about your character.

145
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:38,200
So here is the shift, and it is not a budget.

146
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:41,560
Stop trying to think better inside the tunnel.

147
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:45,150
You cannot. That is the finding, not an excuse.

148
00:09:45,230 --> 00:09:49,910
The farmer with thirteen points more available was not trying harder in the good month,

149
00:09:49,990 --> 00:09:55,820
he simply had them back. What you can do is move the decision to where the capacity is.

150
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:59,340
Look at your own year and find the window where the pressure lifts,

151
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:02,360
even slightly. Payday.

152
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:08,320
The week after something clears. The one afternoon the number is not screaming.

153
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:14,060
That window is your post-harvest month, and it is the only moment your full mind is in the

154
00:10:14,140 --> 00:10:17,380
room. Put the thinking there.

155
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:24,180
The cancellation, the letter, the comparison, the conversation, the plan for the tight week.

156
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:26,760
Not because you will feel stronger then,

157
00:10:27,140 --> 00:10:30,050
but because you will measurably have more mind available then,

158
00:10:30,130 --> 00:10:34,460
and the version of you who is short in three weeks will be running on very little and should not

159
00:10:34,540 --> 00:10:36,960
be asked to decide anything important.

160
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:41,560
You are not moving money. You are moving the decision to the moment when someone is actually

161
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:45,700
home to make it. And nothing about this makes you less short of money.

162
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:47,900
Nothing in these ten minutes does that,

163
00:10:47,980 --> 00:10:52,760
and anyone telling you a mindset closes a real gap is selling you something.

164
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:55,500
What it changes is the story attached to the gap,

165
00:10:55,580 --> 00:10:59,030
and that story has been costing you as much as the fees have.

166
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:03,980
The month you have the least money will probably still be the month you handle it worst.

167
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:06,600
That does not go away tonight.

168
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:09,920
What goes away is the idea that this proves anything about you,

169
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:15,940
because it has now been measured in the same person twice, in the same body, in the same field,

170
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:19,980
and the only thing that changed between the good numbers and the bad ones was how much was in

171
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:26,620
the account. Money is not the only thing that can take a whole mind and refuse to hand it back.

172
00:11:26,700 --> 00:11:31,260
One person you will never meet says one unkind thing about you.

173
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:36,620
Ten other people that day were kind. How much of the evening did the one of them own?

174
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:41,020
That is this same tunnel, opened by something that costs nothing at all,

175
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:44,250
and the video on your screen right now is about that one.

176
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:47,840
Why one stranger's comment can ruin your whole day.

177
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:49,360
Watch that one next.
